config FLASH_BK7258
	bool "Beken BK7258 flash driver"
	default y
	depends on DT_HAS_BEKEN_BK7258_FLASH_ENABLED
	select FLASH
	# Setting CONFIG_FLASH_PAGE_LAYOUT=y in prj.conf alone is not enough:
	# it lives under 'if FLASH_HAS_PAGE_LAYOUT', and that HAS_ symbol has to
	# be selected by the driver itself. Without this select Kconfig drops the
	# assignment and only prints a warning that is easy to miss in the build
	# log.
	select FLASH_HAS_PAGE_LAYOUT
	# Same story: FLASH_HAS_DRIVER_ENABLED gates FLASH_MAP, which MCUboot
	# selects, and a warning there is a build failure.
	select FLASH_HAS_DRIVER_ENABLED
	# Erase really does erase: the service calls through to a sector erase,
	# it is not implied by a write.
	select FLASH_HAS_EXPLICIT_ERASE
	help
	  Flash driver for the Beken BK7258.

	  The defining constraint of this driver is that this core
	  must not issue flash controller commands on its own. Sending a single
	  RDID, a read-only command, resets the whole chip: the device runs in
	  quad / continuous-read mode, and as soon as a command is pushed it
	  leaves that mode while the peer is still fetching address-only from the
	  same device. The peer then executes garbage.

	  Therefore read, write and erase are all proxied to the flash service on
	  the other core over the mailbox, which is what the vendor's own flash
	  client does.

	  Not even a read goes through the XIP window. That window only serves
	  the packed image area, in which every 32 bytes of data are followed
	  by 2 bytes of CRC that the hardware consumes as it fetches, and
	  reading a region that was not written that way resets the chip:
	  measured, physical 0x165000 reads fine through the window while
	  physical 0x290000 and 0x7FA000 reset the board. Whether
	  an offset is safe depends on how the bytes behind it were written,
	  which the driver cannot know, so it never uses the window.

	  Offsets are physical, matching the partition table, which is also what
	  the service expects.

if FLASH_BK7258

config FLASH_BK7258_ERASE_BLOCK_SIZE
	int "Erase block size in physical bytes"
	default 4096
	help
	  Sector granularity of the flash device, used to compute the page
	  layout. This is a count of physical bytes, not payload bytes.
	  The service on the other core erases exactly one of these per
	  request, so an erase spanning several of them is sent as several
	  requests.

config FLASH_BK7258_IPC_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "How long to wait for the flash service, in milliseconds"
	default 3000
	help
	  A programme or erase request is answered by the service only after
	  the flash has been written. Its own wait for a response from this
	  core is 3000 ms, so anything shorter would give up on an operation
	  that is still in flight.

endif # FLASH_BK7258
